     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">hi Kate,<div> you should read this : <a href=\"http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ecy.2093/full\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ecy.2093/full&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451680530000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3zGKm7wdRQ2W_bFNvxMgZ1\">http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ecy.2093/full</a><br></div><div> and maybe a couple related papers cited by Marc Kery and try to answer that question for yourself.  The short answer is that choice of K should be chosen large enough so that it doesn&#39;t have an effect on the result.... if it does then you should reconsider the problem and possibly chose an alternative analysis framework.</div><div>regards</div><div>andy</div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
